00:23:54,880 --> 00:23:56,880
I can bring them around

540
00:23:56,880 --> 00:23:58,880
and get the company straightened out.

541
00:23:58,880 --> 00:24:01,880
So, this is shareholder democracy, how it works well.

542
00:24:01,880 --> 00:24:06,880
And not everyone agrees with Carl Icahn's interpretation.

543
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:11,880
He's a controversial guy.

544
00:24:11,880 --> 00:24:14,380
Now, another thing I want to say,

545
00:24:14,380 --> 00:24:16,880
there are basically two kinds of corporations.

546
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:21,880
There's for-profit and non-profit.

547
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:23,880
I've been talking about for-profit.

548
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:27,380
And all these people listed as owning, all

549
00:24:27,380 --> 00:24:30,880
these people listed, I had on the screen,

550
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:34,880
as listed shares, are for-profit.

551
00:24:34,880 --> 00:24:36,880
That means they have shareholders.

552
00:24:36,880 --> 00:24:38,880
There's another kind of

553
00:24:38,880 --> 00:24:40,880
corporation, which is called non-profit.

554
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:42,880
And we're standing in one right now.

555
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:44,880
It's called Yale University.

556
00:24:44,880 --> 00:24:47,880
It's a non-profit corporation.

557
00:24:47,880 --> 00:24:49,880
And it has a board of directors.

558
00:24:49,880 --> 00:24:51,880
They call it the corporation.

559
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:52,880
They meet regularly.

560
00:24:52,880 --> 00:24:54,880
It's like other corporations.

561
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:56,880
But there are no shareholders.

562
00:24:56,880 --> 00:24:59,880
So, how can that be?

563
00:24:59,880 --> 00:25:01,880
Who owns Yale?

564
00:25:01,880 --> 00:25:05,880
Well, it's a person.

565
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:10,880
Yale University, like other universities, is a legal person.

566
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:13,880
And its rights are defined by law.

567
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:20,380
And the profits go back to Yale and accumulate there

568
00:25:20,380 --> 00:25:26,880
and are spent for a cause, namely education and research.

569
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:29,880
So, the board of directors of Yale,

570
00:25:29,880 --> 00:25:31,880
has a different purpose.

571
00:25:31,880 --> 00:25:34,380
They don't have any duty of loyalty

572
00:25:34,380 --> 00:25:36,880
to shareholders, because there are no shareholders.

573
00:25:36,880 --> 00:25:40,380
I mean, we could call you shareholders, I guess,

574
00:25:40,380 --> 00:25:43,880
if you're students here, but that's not what you're called.

575
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:45,880
And you don't have any rights to the profits.

576
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:48,880
Profits are spent for good causes.

577
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:51,380
So, you kind of wonder, well,

578
00:25:51,380 --> 00:25:53,880
those two sound very different, don't they?

579
00:25:53,880 --> 00:25:57,880
Because a for-profit is trying to make money,

580
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:00,880
and the nonprofit is doing something

581
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:03,880
for a cause that seems totally different.

582
00:26:03,880 --> 00:26:06,380
But they're not always totally different, because

583
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:08,880
a lot of what happens in corporations

584
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:11,880
is determined by kind of

585
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:14,880
the sociology of the organizational structure.

586
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:16,880
So, if you have a board of directors,

587
00:26:16,880 --> 00:26:19,880
you know, some people serve on multiple boards.

588
00:26:19,880 --> 00:26:21,880
They'll be on Yale University,

589
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:25,880
and they'll be on some other company for profit.

590
00:26:25,880 --> 00:26:26,880
But they probably,

591
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:28,880
the same person, they probably behave

592
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:30,880
kind of similarly in both places.

593
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:34,380
So, oddly enough, for-profit and

594
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:37,880
nonprofit don't often mean that much.

595
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:40,380
We're going to come back

596
00:26:40,380 --> 00:26:42,880
to nonprofits later in this semester.

597
00:26:42,880 --> 00:26:46,880
But I'm really talking about for-profit.

598
00:26:46,880 --> 00:26:49,880
Now, that sounds kind of selfish, right?

599
00:26:49,880 --> 00:26:50,880
In a sense.

600
00:26:50,880 --> 00:26:52,380
I kind of like non, well,

601
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:53,880
a lot of us like nonprofits better.

602
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:56,880
They have a different cachet in our society.

603
00:26:56,880 --> 00:26:59,880
I was talking to Peter Tofano,

604
00:26:59,880 --> 00:27:02,880
who's a professor at Harvard Business School,

605
00:27:02,880 --> 00:27:07,880
who set up a nonprofit company called Doorways to Dreams.

606
00:27:07,880 --> 00:27:09,880
That's the name of his company.

607
00:27:09,880 --> 00:27:12,880
There's a website, search on Doorways to Dreams.

608
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:14,880
And it's all about finance for the poor,

609
00:27:14,880 --> 00:27:18,880
and the less represented people.

610
00:27:18,880 --> 00:27:20,880
And he said, setting up a nonprofit

611
00:27:20,880 --> 00:27:22,880
was the greatest thing he ever did,

612
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:24,880
because,

613
00:27:24,880 --> 00:27:27,880
because he,

614
00:27:27,880 --> 00:27:29,880
it opens doors for him.

615
00:27:29,880 --> 00:27:32,380
He walks in and says, I'm running a

