892
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:34,720
rate, and it's not a share in the profits.

893
00:37:34,720 --> 00:37:38,720
So, equity financing tends to attract

894
00:37:38,720 --> 00:37:41,720
investors who take risks.

895
00:37:41,720 --> 00:37:44,220
See, if a company gets debt,

896
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:46,720
then the debt leverages up the

897
00:37:46,720 --> 00:37:48,720
risk of the company.

898
00:37:48,720 --> 00:37:50,720
Because you, as a shareholder,

899
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:52,720
now are paid only after the

900
00:37:52,720 --> 00:37:54,720
debtors are paid.

901
00:37:54,720 --> 00:37:56,720
You own the value of

902
00:37:56,720 --> 00:37:58,720
the company, which is the assets

903
00:37:58,720 --> 00:38:01,720
minus the liabilities, as a shareholder.

904
00:38:01,720 --> 00:38:04,720
But you've got to subtract off the liabilities.

905
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:06,720
So, companies have a choice of

906
00:38:06,720 --> 00:38:08,720
how to, how to get the money

907
00:38:08,720 --> 00:38:11,720
out of their bank, how to finance their operations.

908
00:38:11,720 --> 00:38:14,720
Typically, they start out with equity.

909
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:16,720
I guess they could start out with debt as well.

910
00:38:16,720 --> 00:38:18,220
But in our case, of our

911
00:38:18,220 --> 00:38:19,720
little company, it was all equity at

912
00:38:19,720 --> 00:38:20,720
first.

913
00:38:20,720 --> 00:38:22,720
Then we got bank loans.

914
00:38:22,720 --> 00:38:25,720
And so, we started to get leveraged.

915
00:38:25,720 --> 00:38:30,720
And that's what happens.

916
00:38:30,720 --> 00:38:32,720
Big companies do the same thing.

917
00:38:32,720 --> 00:38:34,220
I like to talk about the

918
00:38:34,220 --> 00:38:35,720
simple company that you might set up

919
00:38:35,720 --> 00:38:37,720
with your friends, because it seems so personal.

920
00:38:37,720 --> 00:38:40,720
And you can imagine doing that, right?

921
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:42,720
And you can imagine the

922
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:44,720
kind of conflicts that would happen.

923
00:38:44,720 --> 00:38:46,220
But these little companies have

924
00:38:46,220 --> 00:38:47,720
a way of growing really, really

925
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:48,720
big.

926
00:38:48,720 --> 00:38:50,220
So, I'm going to talk about

927
00:38:50,220 --> 00:38:51,720
a little company set up by Bill

928
00:38:51,720 --> 00:38:55,720
Gates and Paul Allen in 1975.

929
00:38:55,720 --> 00:38:58,720
Bill Gates was an undergraduate at Harvard.

930
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:02,720
And he saw an opportunity, and he dropped out.

931
00:39:02,720 --> 00:39:04,720
He was right.

932
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:06,720
He never got his Harvard degree.

933
00:39:06,720 --> 00:39:08,720
And he said, I suggest that

934
00:39:08,720 --> 00:39:10,720
if you see such an opportunity,

935
00:39:10,720 --> 00:39:11,720
you do the same thing.

936
00:39:11,720 --> 00:39:13,720
I hate to say it, but

937
00:39:13,720 --> 00:39:15,720
if you can see an opportunity like

938
00:39:15,720 --> 00:39:16,720
that.

939
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:18,720
So, it started out just

940
00:39:18,720 --> 00:39:20,720
Bill and Paul and their little

941
00:39:20,720 --> 00:39:21,720
company.

942
00:39:21,720 --> 00:39:22,720
But they have a way to

943
00:39:22,720 --> 00:39:23,720
grow into bigger and bigger things.

944
00:39:23,720 --> 00:39:27,720
The question is, are they really different later?

945
00:39:27,720 --> 00:39:30,720
Do they?

946
00:39:30,720 --> 00:39:34,720
So, one important question is about whether big companies

947
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:37,720
are still functioning in the way I'm describing.

948
00:39:37,720 --> 00:39:39,720
So, I set the idea of a

949
00:39:39,720 --> 00:39:41,720
company up as a structure that lawyers

950
00:39:41,720 --> 00:39:43,720
invented that allow people to

951
00:39:43,720 --> 00:39:45,720
work together and allow them to

952
00:39:45,720 --> 00:39:47,720
get in and out of the company.

953
00:39:47,720 --> 00:39:49,720
Does it really still work

954
00:39:49,720 --> 00:39:51,720
that way for really big companies?

955
00:39:51,720 --> 00:39:53,720
And there's been a complaint

956
00:39:53,720 --> 00:39:55,720
about the stock market that the

957
00:39:55,720 --> 00:40:00,720
companies generally don't issue any more shares once they're

958
00:40:00,720 --> 00:40:01,720
big.

959
00:40:01,720 --> 00:40:02,720
This is the first time I've heard that.

960
00:40:02,720 --> 00:40:03,720
I don't know.

961
00:40:04,720 --> 00:40:05,720
I'm not saying it's a big deal.

962
00:40:05,720 --> 00:40:06,720
I'm not saying it's a big deal.

963
00:40:06,720 --> 00:40:07,720
I'm just saying that there are some companies that complain,

964
00:40:07,720 --> 00:40:08,720
not saying it's right.

965
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:10,220
And that the stock market ends

966
00:40:10,220 --> 00:40:11,720
up as a market for existing

967
00:40:11,720 --> 00:40:12,720
shares.

968
00:40:12,720 --> 00:40:16,220
And the idea is, is the

969
00:40:16,220 --> 00:40:19,720
stock market, or are the stock markets

970
00:40:19,720 --> 00:40:24,720
of the world functioning to make capitalism work better?

971
00:40:24,720 --> 00:40:26,220
Well, I could say, well, they

972
00:40:26,220 --> 00:40:27,720
should be, because the price of

973
00:40:27,720 --> 00:40:29,720
a share in a company is

974
00:40:29,720 --> 00:40:31,720
an indicator of the value of the

975
00:40:31,720 --> 00:40:33,720
company, and it directs resources.

976
00:40:33,720 --> 00:40:35,220
I mean, people look at that

977
00:40:35,220 --> 00:40:36,720
price, and they decide where to

978
00:40:36,720 --> 00:40:37,720
invest.

979
00:40:37,720 --> 00:40:39,720
But if they're not issuing

980
00:40:39,720 --> 00:40:41,720
any more shares, does it matter?

981
00:40:41,720 --> 00:40:45,720
So, I had a quote from Karl Marx here.

982
00:40:45,720 --> 00:40:49,720
You've heard of Karl Marx, the founder of communism.

983
00:40:49,720 --> 00:40:54,220
And he, in his book

984
00:40:54,220 --> 00:40:58,720
Capital, Das Kapital, his classic work,

985
00:40:58,720 --> 00:41:01,720
said, I'm quoting Marx.

986
00:41:20,720 --> 00:41:21,720
That was Marx's view.

987
00:41:21,720 --> 00:41:22,720
But even in communist countries,

988
00:41:22,720 --> 00:41:23,720
that view doesn't seem to be

989
00:41:23,720 --> 00:41:28,720
holding sway anymore.

990
00:41:28,720 --> 00:41:30,720
The thing that lent some credibility to Marx's work, was

991
00:41:30,720 --> 00:41:31,720
his book, Capitalism.

992
00:41:31,720 --> 00:41:35,040
credibility to these theories is that it often seems that

993
00:41:35,040 --> 00:41:41,500
big companies don't issue many shares anymore.

994
00:41:41,500 --> 00:41:43,220
And I'm going to qualify that.

995
00:41:43,220 --> 00:41:50,060
But there was a very influential article written by

996
00:41:50,060 --> 00:41:59,660
Stuart Meyers, who is a professor of finance at MIT.

997
00:41:59,660 --> 00:42:07,799
And that was in the Journal of Finance, 1984.

998
00:42:07,799 --> 00:42:12,700
And he gave some statistics.

999
00:42:12,700 --> 00:42:15,480
This was an influential article, making it sound like

1000
00:42:15,480 --> 00:42:19,339
shares don't really matter for big companies.

1001
00:42:19,339 --> 00:42:22,500
He said, well, how do companies really finance

1002
00:42:22,500 --> 00:42:29,200
their needs?

1003
00:42:29,200 --> 00:42:29,580
And he gave some statistics.

1004
00:42:29,580 --> 00:42:29,640
And he said, well, how do companies really finance their

1005
00:42:29,640 --> 00:42:30,140
needs?

1006
00:42:30,140 --> 00:42:30,940
And he gave data.

1007
00:42:30,940 --> 00:42:37,660
This is Meyers' data from 1973 to 1982.

1008
00:42:37,660 --> 00:42:47,660
And he found that 62% of corporate financing needs came

1009
00:42:47,660 --> 00:42:51,160
from, in the United States, retained earnings.

1010
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:57,940
What he's saying is, companies, they didn't sell

1011
00:42:57,940 --> 00:42:59,560
new shares.

1012
00:42:59,560 --> 00:43:01,660
They didn't accumulate profits.

1013
00:43:01,660 --> 00:43:02,990
And when they had to build

1014
00:43:02,990 --> 00:43:04,320
a new factory, they went to their

1015
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:06,520
own piggy bank, and they bought the new factory, or

1016
00:43:06,520 --> 00:43:08,740
whatever it was they needed.

1017
00:43:08,740 --> 00:43:13,080
And only 6% was equity-issued.

1018
00:43:13,080 --> 00:43:16,740
This is for the whole U.S. stock market.

1019
00:43:16,740 --> 00:43:18,499
And the rest was debt,

1020
00:43:18,499 --> 00:43:20,259
that they would borrow money for.

1021
00:43:20,259 --> 00:43:22,819
So, Stuart Meyers said, you

1022
00:43:22,819 --> 00:43:25,380
know, all this idea that stock

1023
00:43:25,380 --> 00:43:29,380
markets function to allocate resources, and the share

1024
00:43:29,380 --> 00:43:32,200
market means something is really misleading.

1025
00:43:32,200 --> 00:43:33,700
So, what he proposed is

1026
00:43:33,700 --> 00:43:35,200
what he called the pecking order

1027
00:43:35,200 --> 00:43:36,480
theory.

1028
00:43:40,920 --> 00:43:42,650
What he meant is, companies

1029
00:43:42,650 --> 00:43:44,380
really don't want to issue new

1030
00:43:44,380 --> 00:43:46,960
shares, they just don't want to do it.

1031
00:43:46,960 --> 00:43:48,620
You know, we own this company, we're not

1032
00:43:48,620 --> 00:43:50,580
letting more people in.

1033
00:43:50,580 --> 00:43:52,360
So, what they first do is, they just try to

1034
00:43:52,360 --> 00:43:54,440
save their own money.

1035
00:43:54,440 --> 00:43:56,160
This is Meyer's explanation.

1036
00:43:56,160 --> 00:43:59,360
Companies save money, and when they have money, they spend

1037
00:43:59,360 --> 00:44:02,140
it on expanding.

1038
00:44:02,140 --> 00:44:04,380
And then, that's the highest on the pecking order.

1039
00:44:04,380 --> 00:44:05,800
They like that the best.

1040
00:44:05,800 --> 00:44:07,700
But if they really need money,

1041
00:44:07,700 --> 00:44:09,600
and they can't save enough to

1042
00:44:09,600 --> 00:44:13,220
expand, they have a lot of expansion opportunities.

1043
00:44:13,220 --> 00:44:14,250
They'll go to a bank and

1044
00:44:14,250 --> 00:44:15,280
borrow, or they'll go to an

1045
00:44:15,280 --> 00:44:18,460
investment bank and issue bonds.

1046
00:44:18,460 --> 00:44:21,400
And then, only if it's really, really important, and they

1047
00:44:21,400 --> 00:44:24,080
can't get enough money, they

1048
00:44:24,080 --> 00:44:26,760
will go to selling new shares.

1049
00:44:26,760 --> 00:44:29,340
So, I don't know if Stuart Meyer has said

1050
00:44:29,340 --> 00:44:31,000
that Karl Marx was right, but

1051
00:44:31,000 --> 00:44:32,660
he seemed to be saying that

1052
00:44:32,660 --> 00:44:36,580
equity issuance wasn't that important.

1053
00:44:36,580 --> 00:44:38,900
However, I think that is misleading.

1054
00:44:38,900 --> 00:44:41,100
And I'm going to refer,

1055
00:44:41,100 --> 00:44:43,300
then, to another article that came

1056
00:44:43,300 --> 00:44:47,960
in much later, criticizing Meyer's.

1057
00:44:47,960 --> 00:44:58,540
And the article is by Eugene Fama and Kenneth French.

1058
00:44:59,340 --> 00:45:04,580
That's at Chicago and Dartmouth, respectively.

1059
00:45:04,580 --> 00:45:08,500
They wrote an article in,

1060
00:45:08,500 --> 00:45:12,420
I think it was 2004, 2005.

1061
00:45:12,420 --> 00:45:19,320
And again, this is the Journal of Financial Economics, 2005.

1062
00:45:19,320 --> 00:45:23,540
They said that the problem with Meyer's pecking order theory

1063
00:45:23,540 --> 00:45:27,000
is that Meyer's was looking at aggregate data.

1064
00:45:27,000 --> 00:45:28,070
And he was looking at

1065
00:45:28,070 --> 00:45:29,140
a time period when there was

1066
00:45:29,140 --> 00:45:32,200
relatively little issuance of equity.

1067
00:45:32,200 --> 00:45:36,379
Now, when Meyer said only 6% of corporate financing comes

1068
00:45:36,379 --> 00:45:40,960
from equity issuance, they were talking about net equity.

1069
00:45:40,960 --> 00:45:44,900
That's total new purchases, new sales of shares by

1070
00:45:44,900 --> 00:45:50,240
companies minus repurchases of shares by companies.

1071
00:45:50,240 --> 00:45:53,180
And so, it averaged out to be a low number.

1072
00:45:53,180 --> 00:45:56,160
But in fact, as Fama

1073
00:45:56,160 --> 00:45:59,140
and French point out, in the

1074
00:45:59,140 --> 00:46:04,229
same year that Meyer's study, from

1075
00:46:04,229 --> 00:46:09,319
1973 to 82, 67% of U.S.

1076
00:46:09,319 --> 00:46:14,900
companies issued new shares each year.

1077
00:46:14,900 --> 00:46:20,259
And also, in subsequent years, the percent went up.

1078
00:46:20,259 --> 00:46:27,000
From 1993 to 2002, it was 86%.

1079
00:46:27,000 --> 00:46:28,040
So, that's the problem.

1080
00:46:28,040 --> 00:46:28,980
So, that's the problem.

