so that we don't have to go

1345
00:57:08,400 --> 00:57:09,900
out and issue new equity or borrow money.

1346
00:57:09,900 --> 00:57:11,900
It all sounds reasonable.

1347
00:57:11,900 --> 00:57:14,900
And then, because they don't like to cut a dividend,

1348
00:57:14,900 --> 00:57:16,900
they don't want to adjust upward quickly.

1349
00:57:16,900 --> 00:57:18,900
If earnings shoot up, because if

1350
00:57:18,900 --> 00:57:20,900
they go up and raise their dividend,

1351
00:57:20,900 --> 00:57:22,400
then they might have to cut

1352
00:57:22,400 --> 00:57:23,900
it if earnings go back down.

1353
00:57:23,900 --> 00:57:27,900
So, this is the Lentner model.

1354
00:57:27,900 --> 00:57:30,900
And it is that the dividends

1355
00:57:30,900 --> 00:57:33,900
at time t, year t, let's say,

1356
00:57:33,900 --> 00:57:37,900
minus dividends at year t minus 1,

1357
00:57:37,900 --> 00:57:39,900
equals rho,

1358
00:57:39,900 --> 00:57:43,900
times a number tau, that's the Greek letter tau,

1359
00:57:43,900 --> 00:57:46,900
times earnings per share,

1360
00:57:46,900 --> 00:57:49,900
minus dividend per share,

1361
00:57:49,900 --> 00:57:53,900
at time t minus 1,

1362
00:57:53,900 --> 00:57:56,900
plus noise.

1363
00:57:56,900 --> 00:58:00,900
So, and tau is between 0 and 1.

1364
00:58:00,900 --> 00:58:03,400
So, they have some fraction of

1365
00:58:03,400 --> 00:58:05,900
earnings that they want to pay out.

1366
00:58:05,900 --> 00:58:09,900
And if earnings per share rise above,

1367
00:58:09,900 --> 00:58:11,900
rise so that tau times earnings

1368
00:58:11,900 --> 00:58:13,900
per share is greater than dividends,

1369
00:58:13,900 --> 00:58:15,900
then they increase their dividends.

1370
00:58:15,900 --> 00:58:18,900
And rho is between 0 and 1.

1371
00:58:18,900 --> 00:58:20,900
That's the Lentner model.

1372
00:58:20,900 --> 00:58:23,400
And he showed with the data that

1373
00:58:23,400 --> 00:58:25,900
it pretty much summarizes what firms are doing.

1374
00:58:25,900 --> 00:58:28,900
So, they have a payout ratio out of earnings.

1375
00:58:28,900 --> 00:58:30,900
They think that, and the payout ratio

1376
00:58:30,900 --> 00:58:32,900
has been declining over the last century.

1377
00:58:32,900 --> 00:58:36,900
It used to be typically 60, 70%.

1378
00:58:36,900 --> 00:58:39,900
In 1900, shareholders were impatient with comfort.

1379
00:58:39,900 --> 00:58:41,900
They wanted to see the money.

1380
00:58:41,900 --> 00:58:43,900
But they let them keep 30 to 40%.

1381
00:58:43,900 --> 00:58:45,900
But now, you know, we become more

1382
00:58:45,900 --> 00:58:47,900
and more believers in the stock market,

1383
00:58:47,900 --> 00:58:52,900
and we allow companies to do better things.

1384
00:58:52,900 --> 00:58:57,900
So, I said I would talk about some examples.

